StructuredText.Text:
// Dynamic I/O Mapper: translates each valve's abstract channel-index config
// (Valve_Channels_DB's six flat OpenFbChannel[]/ClosedFbChannel[]/etc arrays) into real feedback/health bits by looking them up in
// IO_Buffer_DB.DI[], and writes commanded outputs into IO_Buffer_DB.DQ[] the same way.
// A channel value of 0 means "not assigned" - that field is simply left untouched, so an
// unconfigured channel never overwrites a valve's simulated status with a false FALSE.
FOR #i := 1 TO 89 DO
    // Which station carries this valve. Confirmed against Valve_Channels_DB rather than
    // assumed: valves 1-27 use DI channels 1-108 (AFT provides 1-112), 28-54 use 113-220
    // (MID provides 113-224), 55-89 use 225-364 (FWD provides 225-368).
    #stationDown := (#i <= 27 AND "Diag_DB".AftLost)
                 OR (#i > 27 AND #i <= 54 AND "Diag_DB".MidLost)
                 OR (#i > 54 AND "Diag_DB".FwdLost);

    // NOT #stationDown is the important half of this guard. When a station is off the network
    // its input data is not a report from the field, and what the process image holds instead
    // is not something this project has been able to establish - Siemens' own documentation
    // was not found to say whether inputs go to zero or freeze at their last value, and it
    // cannot be tested in simulation because PLCSIM runs no PROFINET. Both possibilities are
    // bad in different ways: zeros make every valve on the station report no position and not
    // healthy, which is ~54 alarms burying the one that explains them; frozen values make a
    // dead station look perfectly normal.
    //
    // Skipping the copy removes the question. Valve[i].OpenFB/ClosedFB/Healthy/LocalMode
    // simply keep the values they last had, which is the last real report from the field -
    // the thing an operator actually needs when a station drops, since knowing whether a
    // ballast line was open or shut is the first question asked. And because Healthy stays
    // TRUE and the position stays valid, the Unhealthy and Loss-of-Position alarms do not
    // fire for the whole station, leaving the RIO station alarm standing on its own.
    //
    // Travel timeouts are deliberately NOT suppressed. If someone commands a valve on a dead
    // station, nothing moves and FailOpen/FailClose is the correct thing to say - that is one
    // alarm about one deliberate action, not a flood.
    IF "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].Configured AND NOT #stationDown THEN
        IF "Valve_Channels_DB".OpenFbChannel[#i] > 0 THEN
            "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].OpenFB := "IO_Buffer_DB".DI["Valve_Channels_DB".OpenFbChannel[#i]];
        END_IF;
        IF "Valve_Channels_DB".ClosedFbChannel[#i] > 0 THEN
            "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].ClosedFB := "IO_Buffer_DB".DI["Valve_Channels_DB".ClosedFbChannel[#i]];
        END_IF;
        IF "Valve_Channels_DB".HealthyChannel[#i] > 0 THEN
            "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].Healthy := "IO_Buffer_DB".DI["Valve_Channels_DB".HealthyChannel[#i]];
        END_IF;
        // Double-indication safety trip, re-applied here on purpose: FB_ValveLoop already
        // checks this earlier in the same scan, but for a real-channel valve this Healthy
        // write above runs AFTER that check and would silently undo it - a real valve with
        // both limit switches made (physically impossible, a wiring/sensor fault) would show
        // Healthy=TRUE if the real Healthy channel itself doesn't happen to also be false.
        // Found by reasoning through the watch-table test 2026-08-14, not observed live (that
        // test only forced OpenFB, never both at once) - fixing before it's ever hit for real.
        IF "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].OpenFB AND "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].ClosedFB THEN
            "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].Healthy := FALSE;
        END_IF;
        IF "Valve_Channels_DB".LocalModeChannel[#i] > 0 THEN
            "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].LocalMode := "IO_Buffer_DB".DI["Valve_Channels_DB".LocalModeChannel[#i]];
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
    // Outputs are driven UNCONDITIONALLY - deliberately outside the Configured guard above.
    // That guard exists to protect a simulated valve's feedbacks from being overwritten by a
    // real DI; it has no business gating the outputs. When it did, de-configuring a valve
    // mid-travel froze its DQ bit at whatever it was last written to, and FC_PhysicalIoCopy
    // kept copying that stale TRUE to the real %Q forever - a relay energised with nothing
    // managing it. FB_ValveLoop now forces OpenValve/CloseValve FALSE for any unconfigured
    // valve, so writing DQ every scan regardless is what actually de-energises the output.
    IF "Valve_Channels_DB".OpenCmdChannel[#i] > 0 THEN
        "IO_Buffer_DB".DQ["Valve_Channels_DB".OpenCmdChannel[#i]] := "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].OpenValve;
    END_IF;
    IF "Valve_Channels_DB".CloseCmdChannel[#i] > 0 THEN
        "IO_Buffer_DB".DQ["Valve_Channels_DB".CloseCmdChannel[#i]] := "Valves_DB".Valve[#i].CloseValve;
    END_IF;
END_FOR;
